WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.300
In this section we'll cover adding the logic to our Blueprint to allow for player input once the sequence has paused.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:16.329
So back over in our project, let's go ahead and add the logic to our Event Begin Play, particularly between our Pause and Destroy Actor.

3
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:26.640
So I'm going to go ahead and just disconnect our Destroy Actor, which you can hold down the Alt key and left click, and we'll just move this kind of out of the way.

4
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.640
So after the Pause node, let's go ahead and add a Gate.

5
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:38.640
And from the Gate node, I'm going to go ahead and move our Enter, so from the Pause we don't want it to enter, we actually want it to toggle.

6
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:45.090
Now I can hold down Ctrl, click the little noodle here, and move it to our toggle.

7
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:54.090
And then on the Gate itself, on Enter, let's go ahead and just drag backwards, and I'm going to type in the letter P and start typing in Keyboard.

8
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.090
That should give us the P key.

9
00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:04.090
And essentially what this is going to do is open our Gate or toggle our Gate once the P key is pressed.

10
00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:09.090
And this is what we're going to use to unpause our sequence when the player inputs.

11
00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:16.090
Alright, so from the exit on our Gate, let's go ahead and drag off of this and add a Play.

12
00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:21.090
And what we're looking for is we can do Play Sequence, and it may show up, it may not.

13
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:27.090
Uncheck Context Sensitive, and there we go, we're going to do our Not Play Sequence, but Play.

14
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.540
And this is just going to continue to play the sequence that we have.

15
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.540
Alright, now from here, our target is still going to be the main sequence.

16
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:44.540
Now we could drag off of this, we could select it, Ctrl C, or again we can grab our main sequence here, drag it onto the target,

17
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.540
and you'll see that it automatically creates our translator and our variable.

18
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:55.540
It is essentially the same as what we have here, just we're recalling it and making our graph a little cleaner.

19
00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:02.430
Alright, and then let's go ahead and reconnect after here, our Destroy Actor, we'll hit Compile.

20
00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:07.430
And then one other thing that we need to change real fast is if we leave this logic as is,

21
00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:13.430
the problem is because this is an actor in the level, and we have this P key,

22
00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.430
it's not really sure who is pressing this particular key.

23
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.430
So to do that, we're going to go up to Class Defaults, and then over on the side here,

24
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.430
we're actually going to search for Auto Receive Input, and you'll see here it's disabled.

25
00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:34.430
We need to change this to Player 0, and Player 0 is just the default player that is playing the game.

26
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:40.430
So we'll change this to Player 0, and again doing this we'll make sure that when the P key on the keyboard is pressed,

27
00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:46.430
or any key, that it's recognizing that it's from the actual player, not a random one.

28
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.430
After that, hit Compile and Save.
